If someone wants a workaround - you can ran a separate waybar for tray and nothing else, and toggle it on hotkey.

<details><summary>Details</summary>
<p>

~/.config/waybar/tray.jsonc

```jsonc
{
  "layer": "top",
  "position": "right", // Waybar position (top|bottom|left|right)
  "exclusive": false, // don't reserve space for bar, open over existing windows
  "height": 3, // height so short so that the invisible part doesn't extend beyond the tray
               // (the tray will grow when more icons are added without issues still)
  "width": 42,
  "modules-center": ["tray"],
  "tray": {
    // "icon-size": 21,
    "spacing": 10
  }
}

```

~/.config/waybar/tray.css

```css
@define-color background #f8e1e1;
@define-color foreground rgba(27, 22, 22, 0.85);

* {
  border: none;
  border-radius: 0;
  font-family: Monospace;
  font-size: 14px;
}

window#waybar {
  background: transparent;
}

#tray {
  opacity: 0.6;
  margin-bottom: 4px;
  margin-top: 4px;

  margin-left: 3px;

  padding-top: 16px;
  padding-bottom: 16px;

  /* padding-left: 16px; */
  /* padding-right: 16px; */
  transition: none;
  color: @foreground;
  background: @background;
  border-radius: 26px;
}

```

Then you can send `SIGUSR1` signals to waybar to toggle visibility

```bash
pkill --signal SIGUSR1 -f 'bin.*waybar.*tray'
```

alternatively, if you run it as systemd service

```bash
systemctl --user kill -s SIGUSR1 your-waybar-tray.service
```

It will look like

![waybar-tray](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e44f0e44-dc9e-490f-86da-b21937e33515)


</p>
</details>

